data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?** is still open, and I haven't resolved it. The human decides it by clicking an option in Asks, or by telling me here.\n\n**Simply put:** A cart has grown too big to live in the 4 KB session cookie, so it needs to be stored on the server. The question is which database holds it.\n\n**A concrete example:** A shopper adds 30 items across several visits. The cart is now larger than the cookie can hold, so the server has to remember it under the shopper's ID. Whichever store we pick is where that record lives.\n\n**What each option leads to:**\n\n- **Redis (a1):** Carts sit in memory. Reads and writes are very fast, and carts can expire automatically after a set time, such as 30 days. The risk is durability. If Redis restarts, is flushed, or evicts keys under memory pressure, those carts disappear, and shoppers lose their items.\n- **Postgres (a2):** Carts are stored with the rest of the app's data. They survive restarts and evictions, and you can query them, for example to find abandoned carts. The cost is slightly slower reads than Redis, and cleanup of old carts needs a scheduled job instead of a built-in expiry.\n\n**My recommendation: Postgres (a2), which matches the main agent's recommendation.** For a shopping cart, losing items is a worse failure than a few extra milliseconds per request. Postgres also avoids running a second data store if the app doesn't already use Redis. Choose Redis instead only if carts are truly disposable and speed matters more than keeping them.\n\n**What I couldn't check:** The main agent's checkout has no tracked files, so I couldn't confirm whether the app already uses Redis or Postgres. If it already runs Postgres, that makes the case for a2 stronger. If it already runs Redis and nothing else, a1 becomes more reasonable.\n\nWaiting on the human's choice for **A1**. Once they pick an option, I'll resolve it with that actionId and a note for the main agent."}}

